From b12441dc1b17495374bd91bb7622aefc18d1769c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ajhochy <ajhochy@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 23:05:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] fix(desktop): wire the Windows guardian resolver into
 production and close S1 gaps

Replaces the injected-only Windows Job guardian artifact resolver with a
concrete production implementation (windows-guardian-resolver.ts): real
canonical-path/reparse checks, secure ACL checks on the staged exe and its
containing directory, real SHA-256/PE-machine parsing, production
Authenticode policy gated on packaged vs. dev, and no cached validation
result across launches. It is wired as the default for
createWindowsOwnedRuntimeDeps() via a new guardianDir option, so
createOpenDesignRuntimeSupervisor() is no longer a factory a caller can only
exercise with hand-rolled fakes -- a real, disk-backed, non-injected
end-to-end composition test now proves resolver -> guardian handshake ->
Windows readiness -> graceful/forced stop through the exported factory with
no substitute for any of it.

That composition work surfaced a real defect: createWindowsOwnedRuntimeDeps
wired Windows readiness's requestStatus to the guardian's own protocol
STATUS response (state/pid/creationFiletime), but waitForOpenDesignReadyWindows
requires the desktop sidecar's IPC status shape (pid/state/windowVisible/url).
Every real Windows launch would have polled the wrong shape and timed out
waiting for readiness. Fixed to query the desktop sidecar over IPC, matching
stopWindowsOwnedRuntime's existing pattern.

Other repair-packet reconciliation:
- guardian.cpp: check InitializeProcThreadAttributeList's sizing-call result,
  check the three previously-unchecked TerminateJobObject calls in
  CreateThread-failure cleanup paths, and reject a blank stdin control line
  instead of tolerating it as a no-op.
- build-windows-job-guardian.mjs: verify the just-compiled binary is a
  genuine x64 PE image before writing its manifest, rather than trusting a
  successful compiler exit code alone.
- stage.mjs: isValidPublishedRuntime no longer trusts provenance.json's five
  fields alone when reusing an existing immutable publication -- it now
  re-runs the same full payload validator a fresh extraction would run
  (validateWindowsAppPayload for win32, a real codesign --verify plus the
  complete native closure for darwin via the newly extracted
  validateMacAppPayload), so a tampered/incomplete destination that retains
  valid provenance fields fails closed instead of being reused indefinitely.
- .nvmrc and the Windows guardian workflow moved from Node 26 to Node 22,
  matching the frozen contract's Node 22.22+ requirement.

Local gates: focused suites for every touched/new module, npm run
test:open-design (378/378), npm run test:desktop:platforms (1872/1874, 2
pre-existing skips), typecheck, changed-file + full quiet lint, build,
postbuild, actionlint, git diff --check, an added-line secret/eval/shell
scan, a re-run of the existing guardian race probe, and a real mac-arm64
staging audit (genuine codesign --verify against the already-staged signed
app) all pass. Real MSVC compilation, native Job lifecycle/crash execution,
Authenticode verification, and installed NSIS/MSI smoke remain mandatory,
unexecuted external gates on a real Windows x64 host.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
